    <article class="ba-body">
      <p>Ashwagandha (Withania somnifera) is a small evergreen shrub. Its root has been part of traditional Ayurvedic practice for centuries, where it is classed as a rasayana — a group of herbs traditionally used to support overall vitality.</p><p>Our Ashwagandha Powder is made from the root only, milled into a fine, earthy powder.</p>
      <h2 id="highlights">Highlights</h2><ul class="ba-hl"><li>Botanical name: Withania somnifera. Part used: root.</li><li>Traditionally taken in warm milk in the evening.</li><li>Researchers have studied ashwagandha in small clinical trials; NCCIH notes the evidence is still limited.</li><li>Not recommended during pregnancy — talk to your healthcare professional first.</li></ul>
      <h2 id="s0">Traditional use</h2><p>In Ayurveda, ashwagandha root is traditionally taken with warm milk or ghee, often in the evening as part of a wind-down routine.</p><h2 id="s1">What research says</h2><p>The U.S. National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH) summarises that ashwagandha has been studied in small trials, and that more research is needed. It also lists possible side effects and people who should avoid it.</p><h2 id="s2">Using it at home</h2><p>Whisk a teaspoon into warm oat or dairy milk with cinnamon and a little honey — our golden moon milk recipe is a good place to start.</p>
      
